Register cleanups for the objects the RBAC and federation tests create

Many test_identity_delete_* tests of keystone-tempest-plugin 0.22.0
create an object with the system-admin client, check that the persona
under test may not delete it, and register no cleanup, so the object
stays in Keystone. One run of the keystone include list of
tests/tempest/keystone-2026-1 (2070 tests, 4 workers, 0 failures) left
78 objects in a Keystone that nothing else wrote to: 9 catalog services
without an endpoint, 9 policies, 9 OAuth1 consumers, 9 regions, 9
global roles, 9 domain roles, 5 groups, 3 projects, 15 domains and 1
federation mapping. The 2026.2 list leaves the same 78. `tempest
cleanup` removes none of the services, policies, consumers and groups,
so a cloud that runs Tempest every night gathers them, and the leaked
services grow the catalog of every token (#1841).

The patch registers the missing cleanup at 17 places in 12 files,
directly after the create call and before the first request of the
persona, and keeps every check the tests make. Each method runs in
every persona class that inherits it, which is why one place accounts
for up to 9 objects.

Two Keystone behaviours turn a missing cleanup into a lasting object.
Keystone creates a domain for an identity provider that names none and
keeps it when the identity provider is deleted.
IdentityV3RbacProtocolTests.setUpClass creates such a provider for each
of its 11 persona classes, so the patch also disables and deletes the
provider's domain; cleanups run last in, first out, and the domain goes
after the provider. Deleting a domain leaves its domain roles, so
test_identity_delete_domain_role deletes its role itself. A domain is
disabled before it is deleted, the order IdentityV3RbacGroupTest.setUp
uses.

Four places left no object on the measured run. The group of
DomainMemberTests.test_identity_delete_group and the user of
DomainMemberTests.test_identity_delete_user live in the persona's own
domain, which Tempest's dynamic credentials delete with its contents.
The domain config of test_identity_delete_domain_config goes with the
domain its class deletes. Keystone deletes an endpoint with its
service, and tests/tempest/keystone-2026-1 excludes
test_identity_delete_endpoint. They carry the same defect, and the
domain roles show that deleting a domain does not remove everything in
it, so the patch covers them too.

`test_keystone_plugin_registers_cleanups` in
tests/container-images/verify_tempest.sh checks the patched files in
the built image.

nix/lib/python-env.nix applies the patch to the installed wheel of the
two tempest locks with `patch --fuzz=0`. A plugin release that moves
these lines fails the build there with "Hunk #N FAILED", and a release
that carries the fix fails it with "Reversed (or previously applied)
patch detected".

Applies to keystone-tempest-plugin 0.22.0 (releases/2026.1/test-refs.yaml, releases/2026.2/test-refs.yaml).

Upstream status: not submitted; tracked in #1840.

